<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>New chronic kidney disease, probably G3b A2, of uncertain cause in a 68-year-old Black woman with tiredness, ankle swelling and high blood pressure readings: race-free eGFR 40 by creatinine and 38 with cystatin C, and a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio of 240 mg/g. Please advise on the cause and further evaluation, APOL1 genetic testing, kidney-protective treatment and its order, the anemia and mineral findings, and how and when to plan for the future. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within two weeks, on the day of her two-week visit, after the repeat urine test and the kidney ultrasound. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What is the most likely cause of her kidney disease, and does she need further tests, including a kidney biopsy?</item><item>Do you agree with the race-free staging, and how should we use cystatin C from here?</item><item>Should she be offered APOL1 genetic testing, and how would a result change her care?</item><item>Which kidney-protective medicines do you advise, in what order, and with what blood pressure target?</item><item>How should we manage the mild anemia, the low vitamin D and the raised PTH?</item><item>How often should she be seen by nephrology, and when should planning for kidney replacement begin?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mrs. Wells, 68, is seen at the request of Dr. Ashworth-Banks for chronic kidney disease found two weeks ago. She has been tired for some months and has had ankle swelling by evening. She took naproxen for knee aching for about a year and stopped it two weeks ago. She has no diabetes, no visible blood in the urine, no foamy urine, no rash, joint swelling or mouth ulcers, and no history of kidney stones or urinary infections. She brought an outside report from two years ago: creatinine 1.05 mg/dL with an eGFR of 64 reported as normal, from an equation with a race coefficient. Her home blood pressure average over seven days is 150/88. She asked detailed questions and wants the evidence behind each recommendation. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Ida B. Wells was born enslaved on July 16, 1862, in Holly Springs, Mississippi, during the Civil War. [D]</item><item>In the yellow fever epidemic of 1878, when she was 16, both her parents and an infant brother died; she left school and took on the care of her surviving younger siblings, teaching to support them. [D]</item><item>She was an investigative journalist and editor who documented lynching in the United States, notably in A Red Record (1895), and campaigned against it for the rest of her life; she was also a suffragist and civil rights leader. [D]</item><item>On June 27, 1895, she married the Chicago attorney Ferdinand L. Barnett, kept her own name alongside his, and settled in Chicago; they had four children. [D]</item><item>She signed the call for the 1909 conference that led to the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People and is considered one of its founders, though she was left off the list of its founding committee and later drew away from it. [D]</item><item>In 1930 she ran, unsuccessfully, for the Illinois State Senate. [D]</item><item>She began writing her autobiography in her last years; it was published after her death as Crusade for Justice: The Autobiography of Ida B. Wells, edited by her youngest daughter, Alfreda M. Duster. [D]</item></list><paragraph>2 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

