<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Chronic kidney disease G3b A2 (N18.32). Chronic kidney disease G3b A2, present for at least two years and falling by about 10 mL/min a year. The bland urine, moderate albuminuria, small echogenic kidneys and left ventricular hypertrophy point to hypertensive kidney disease; APOL1-associated kidney disease is possible in a woman of African ancestry with this picture. There is no sign of diabetic, glomerular inflammatory or monoclonal disease, and naproxen may have added to the decline. A biopsy is not indicated now; it would be if blood appeared in the urine, albuminuria rose toward the nephrotic range, or eGFR fell quickly despite treatment. The race-free staging is correct: the older equation would have read the earlier result as normal and delayed recognition, which is the harm the NKF-ASN Task Force recommendation was written to end. Use creatinine for routine monitoring and add cystatin C when a decision depends on accuracy, such as drug dosing or transplant evaluation. [S]</item><item>Hypertension with chronic kidney disease (I12.9). Stage 2 hypertension, confirmed at home, with target organ damage. An ARB is the foundation with this albuminuria. Goal below 130/80, and a systolic below 120 by standardized measurement if she tolerates it without dizziness. [S]</item><item>Anemia of chronic kidney disease (D63.1). Mild and iron replete; no erythropoiesis-stimulating agent is indicated. Recheck in three months. [S]</item><item>Vitamin D deficiency with a mildly raised PTH (E55.9). Correct the vitamin D deficiency first; the PTH may settle. No calcitriol or phosphate binder. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Intake results as listed by Dr. Ashworth-Banks. Recalculated earlier value: eGFR 59 by the race-free CKD-EPI 2021 equation (64 by the older race-based equation). Today&apos;s results by the older equation would read 44. Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 228 mg/g today (240 at intake). Urinalysis: 1+ protein, no blood, no casts; bland sediment. Serum protein electrophoresis: no monoclonal protein; free light chain ratio 1.2, normal for her eGFR. Hepatitis B surface antigen, hepatitis C antibody and HIV negative; hepatitis B surface antibody negative. Kidney ultrasound this morning: right kidney 9.6 cm, left 9.4 cm, both echogenic with thinned cortex, no hydronephrosis, stones or masses. ECG with voltage criteria for left ventricular hypertrophy. Kidney failure risk equation: about 7% at 5 years, 2% at 2 years. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Losartan 50 mg daily now, with potassium and creatinine in two weeks; accept a creatinine rise of up to 30%. [S]</item><item>Then dapagliflozin 10 mg daily, which KDIGO recommends at her eGFR with albuminuria of 200 mg/g or more, with a further check at two to four weeks; then increase losartan to 100 mg if potassium stays below 5.5 mmol/L. Finerenone is not indicated without diabetes. [S]</item><item>Sick-day rules in writing: hold losartan and dapagliflozin on days of vomiting, diarrhea or not eating, and before surgery or contrast. [S]</item><item>No NSAIDs; acetaminophen for knee pain. [S]</item><item>Atorvastatin 20 mg daily, as KDIGO advises for adults 50 or older with eGFR below 60. [S]</item><item>Vitamin D3 1,000 units daily; PTH, vitamin D and hemoglobin in three months. [S]</item><item>Hepatitis B vaccination, since she is not immune, with the other vaccines her primary clinician has planned. [S]</item><item>APOL1 testing offered after counseling: a result would not change her medicines or blood pressure target now, may inform her risk and any future transplant evaluation, and has implications for relatives; the federal genetic nondiscrimination law does not cover life or long-term care insurance. She chose to defer, and may ask at any time. [S]</item><item>Kidney education session now on the treatment options should her kidneys fail, and a dietitian session (sodium below 2 g a day, protein about 0.8 g per kg). [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Ashworth-Banks the same day. The calculations behind each eGFR, the risk estimate and the reasons for each medicine were shown to Mrs. Wells, who asked questions and agreed to start losartan and to the plan for dapagliflozin. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Nephrology review in three months with the urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio, then every three to six months while eGFR is below 45 and falling. Transplant evaluation and discussion of dialysis access or conservative care when eGFR approaches 20 or the 2-year kidney failure risk passes 10%, earlier if she wishes. Sooner for potassium above 5.5 mmol/L, a fall in eGFR of more than 30% after starting medicines, or blood in the urine. Dr. Ashworth-Banks continues primary care and monitoring. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
